A pad's Home (PS) and Share buttons go to the game instead of macOS's Game Overlay, Launchpad and screenshots.

This repository's patch to the Mac driver, from neutron's patches/wine/0017-winebus-sdl-gamepads.patch
(dlls/winemac.drv/cocoa_gamepad.m, with its hooks in neutron's 0010 and 0014; github.com/fourlexboehm/neutron at
0ac2574d, LGPL-2.1-or-later, Copyright 2026 the neutron contributors), with the variable renamed.

Games read pads in winedevice (winebus through SDL, patch 0035), a background process. macOS binds a pad's Home or
PS button and its Share or Create button to system gestures and applies them for the frontmost app, which is the
game's Wine process; SDL turns them off only in its own process (SDL_mfijoystick.m). So each Wine process with the
Mac driver asks GameController to leave every button bound to a system gesture to the app
(preferredSystemGestureState = GCSystemGestureStateDisabled), for the pads connected now and each one connected
later, on the main queue, as a native game does. MAVRIK_PAD_SYSTEM_GESTURES=1 keeps the macOS gestures. winemac.so
links GameController (macOS 11 and later; the engine's target is 14.0).

Checked (mavrik S217 lab): applies after patches 0001 to 0035 with no fuzz and winemac.so builds with no new
warning. Its effect needs a real pad and the screen, so it is proven in mavrik's walk.

